            <title><![CDATA[Private German rocket makes history, reaches orbit from European soil (117 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/isar-aerospace-second-launch-norway-andoya-spaceport-spectrum-rocket</link>
            <guid>49580369</guid>
            <pubDate>Sat, 05 Sep 2026 20:31:38 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/isar-aerospace-second-launch-norway-andoya-spaceport-spectrum-rocket">https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/isar-aerospace-second-launch-norway-andoya-spaceport-spectrum-rocket</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49580369">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div data-widget-type="contentparsed" id="content">
<section>

<div>
<figure>
<picture data-new-v2-image="true">
<source type="image/webp" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1920-80.jpg.webp 1920w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1600-80.jpg.webp 1600w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1200-80.jpg.webp 1200w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1024-80.jpg.webp 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-970-80.jpg.webp 970w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-750-80.jpg.webp 750w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-650-80.jpg.webp 650w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-480-80.jpg.webp 480w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-320-80.jpg.webp 320w" sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 600px, 100vw">
<img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY.jpg" alt="a white rocket launches into a dark night sky from a seaside pad with mountains in the background" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1920-80.jpg 1920w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1600-80.jpg 1600w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1200-80.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-1024-80.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-970-80.jpg 970w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-750-80.jpg 750w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-650-80.jpg 650w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-480-80.jpg 480w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY-320-80.jpg 320w" sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 600px, 100vw" data-new-v2-image="true" data-original-mos="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY.jpg" data-pin-media="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/K5YFiwFEXDGQeTecwrNxcY.jpg" data-pin-nopin="true" fetchpriority="high" data-component-name="Image">
</picture>
<figcaption> <span>Isar Aerospace's second Spectrum rocket launches from Andoya Space Center in Norway on Sept. 5, 2026.</span>
<span>(Image credit: Isar Aerospace/NASASpaceflight)</span>
</figcaption>
</figure></div>
<div id="article-body">

<p id="elk-d391eb92-a7a4-11f1-87d8-439982f7dd87">No rocket had ever reached orbit from European soil — until today</p><p>The German company Isar Aerospace launched its Spectrum rocket from <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.space.com/europe-spaceport-continental-Norway-operational" data-url="https://www.space.com/europe-spaceport-continental-Norway-operational" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.space.com/europe-spaceport-continental-Norway-operational"><u>Andøya Space Center</u></a> in northern Norway today (Sept. 5) at 4:12 p.m. EDT (2012 GMT; 10:12 p.m. local time in Norway). A little over seven minutes later, it etched its name into the record books, settling into an elliptical path around our planet.</p><p>"This is right now making history — making history for Spectrum, making history for Isar, making history for Europe,” Nikolaos Perakis, Spectrum chief engineer at Isar, said during today's launch webcast, which the company produced in collaboration with <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.youtube.com/@NASASpaceflight" target="_blank" data-url="https://www.youtube.com/@NASASpaceflight" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link"><u>NASASpaceflight</u></a>. "I'm incredibly proud of this massive achievement."</p><figure data-bordeaux-image-check="" id="elk-d9c3d9b4-a969-11f1-856d-4ffefb9e1811"><div><p> <picture data-new-v2-image="true">
<source type="image/webp" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-1200-80.jpg.webp 1200w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-1024-80.jpg.webp 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-970-80.jpg.webp 970w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-650-80.jpg.webp 650w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-480-80.jpg.webp 480w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-320-80.jpg.webp 320w" sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 970px, calc(100vw - 40px)">
<img src="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d.jpg" alt="view from a rocket stage looking down at a cloudy earth" srcset="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-1200-80.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-1024-80.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-970-80.jpg 970w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-650-80.jpg 650w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-480-80.jpg 480w, https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d-320-80.jpg 320w" sizes="(min-width: 1000px) 970px, calc(100vw - 40px)" loading="lazy" data-new-v2-image="true" data-original-mos="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d.jpg" data-pin-media="https://cdn.mos.cms.futurecdn.net/E3pR43vLrry7WuReSVQq7d.jpg">
</picture></p></div><figcaption itemprop="caption description"><span>The view from the second stage of Isar Aerospace's Spectrum rocket after it reached orbit on Sept. 5, 2026. </span><span itemprop="copyrightHolder">(Image credit: Isar Aerospace/NASASpaceflight)</span></figcaption></figure><p id="elk-d391ed54-a7a4-11f1-955c-45a4a6cb0a3a">This was Spectrum's second bite at the apple: The two-stage, 95-foot-tall (28-meter-tall) <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.space.com/29295-rocket-history.html" data-url="https://www.space.com/29295-rocket-history.html" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.space.com/29295-rocket-history.html"><u>rocket</u></a> first flew in March 2025, lifting off from Andøya on a flight Isar called "Going Full Spectrum." But, as often happens on debut missions, Spectrum suffered an anomaly that day; it came <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/1st-ever-orbital-rocket-launch-from-european-soil-falls-to-earth-and-explodes-seconds-into-flight" data-url="https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/1st-ever-orbital-rocket-launch-from-european-soil-falls-to-earth-and-explodes-seconds-into-flight" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.space.com/space-exploration/launches-spacecraft/1st-ever-orbital-rocket-launch-from-european-soil-falls-to-earth-and-explodes-seconds-into-flight"><u>crashing back to Earth</u></a> less than a minute after launch.</p><p id="elk-d391ed54-a7a4-11f1-955c-45a4a6cb0a3a-1">"A comprehensive investigation was completed within two months of the launch," Isar wrote in a <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://isaraerospace.com/mission-updates-overview" target="_blank" data-url="https://isaraerospace.com/mission-updates-overview" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link"><u>September 2025 update</u></a>. "The findings identified an unintended opening of the vent valve and a loss of attitude control at the start of the roll maneuver as the initiating events that led to the in-flight failure."</p><p>The company took the lessons learned that day and geared up for flight number two, "Onward and Upward," which was originally supposed to launch in January.</p><p>An issue with a pressurization valve nixed that plan, however, pushing the flight to March. Bad weather and an intruding boat scuttled multiple attempts that month, and an April 9 try was scrubbed due to a leak in a composite overwrapped pressure vessel.</p><p>Isar next tried to fly on June 15 but stood down after "detecting off-nominal behavior in the vehicle's fluid systems," according to a company <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://x.com/isaraerospace/status/2066598242020700345" target="_blank" data-url="https://x.com/isaraerospace/status/2066598242020700345" referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link"><u>update that day on X</u></a>. But the company got that issue under control, rolling Spectrum back to the pad for today's launch.</p><p>"We had some delays, obviously," Petrakis said during the webcast. "We had some setbacks, but we came back stronger from them. And it validates this tremendous amount of engineering across all the teams, and that's what brings us into orbit."</p><p id="elk-d391ee30-a7a4-11f1-8c89-61edda84fe3a">Spectrum didn't carry viable payloads on its debut flight, but it did today: Five <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.space.com/34324-cubesats.html" data-url="https://www.space.com/34324-cubesats.html" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.space.com/34324-cubesats.html"><u>cubesats</u></a> and one non-deployable scientific experiment went up on "Onward and Upward."</p><p>Spectrum's upper stage needs to deploy those payloads before Isar can declare full mission success. And deployment won't occur until after the rocket performs a circularization burn to get out of its initial elliptical orbit, which was estimated to feature a closest approach to Earth of 112 miles (180 kilometers) and a farthest-away point of 311 miles (500 km).</p><p>Whether or not Spectrum checks those boxes, today was a big day for Isar, which is thinking big with Spectrum. The company sees the rocket — which can haul about 2,200 pounds (1,000 kilograms) of payload to <a data-analytics-id="inline-link" href="https://www.space.com/low-earth-orbit" data-url="https://www.space.com/low-earth-orbit" data-hl-processed="none" data-article-category="space-exploration" data-mrf-recirculation="inline-link" data-before-rewrite-localise="https://www.space.com/low-earth-orbit"><u>low Earth orbit</u></a> — becoming a workhorse launcher of small to medium-size satellites down the road, and its infrastructure supports that vision. Isar says its manufacturing facility near Munich can churn out more than 30 of the rockets per year.</p><p>“Flight 2 is a big, big milestone, big success," Petrakis said. "But this is just the beginning. So I'm really looking forward to the next steps."</p><p><em><strong>Editor's note:</strong></em><em> This story was updated at 4:45 p.m. ET with news of successful launch and orbital insertion.</em></p>
</div>






<div data-author-bio-max-height="250" data-author-bio-read-more="Read more" data-author-bio-read-less="Read less" id="slice-container-authorBio-tL7L4w59aPoVP347Bmtqef" data-component-name="Slice:AuthorBio">
<p>Michael Wall is the Spaceflight and Tech Editor for <a href="https://www.space.com/" target="_blank" data-before-normalise-host="http://space.com/" data-before-rewrite-localise="http://www.space.com/">Space.com</a> and joined the team in 2010. He primarily covers human and robotic spaceflight, military space, and exoplanets, but has been known to dabble in the space art beat. His book about the search for alien life, "Out There," was published on Nov. 13, 2018. Before becoming a science writer, Michael worked as a herpetologist and wildlife biologist. He has a Ph.D. in evolutionary biology from the University of Sydney, Australia, a bachelor's degree from the University of Arizona, and a graduate certificate in science writing from the University of California, Santa Cruz. To find out what his latest project is, you can follow Michael on Twitter.</p>
</div>
</section>



</div></div>]]></description>
